<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  v108.0 — Android 12+ (API 31) Material You palette, LIGHT.

  On API 31+ the platform exposes a tonal palette derived from the user's
  wallpaper. Using it is what makes a widget sit on the home screen as part of it
  rather than as a sticker on top, and it is the reason the card follows the
  system theme for free: the launcher inflates these in ITS process, with ITS
  configuration.

  TONE DIRECTION: Android's system_* ramps run 0 = white to 1000 = black, so a
  LIGHT surface takes a low tone and its ink takes a high one. That is inverted
  in values-night-v31. Getting this backwards yields white-on-white, which is why
  the render test shoots both themes rather than trusting the naming.

  CONTRAST IS GUARANTEED BY TONAL DISTANCE, NOT BY A HEX WE CAN MEASURE. These
  resolve from the wallpaper at runtime, so test-widget.js cannot compute a ratio
  for them the way it does for the values/ pair. What it CAN pin, and does, is
  that every tone pairing here keeps a gap of at least 500 between surface and
  foreground — the Material 3 tonal spec puts that comfortably past 4.5:1 for any
  hue. The rendered PNGs from WidgetRenderTest are the empirical check.

  BRAND TRADE-OFF, STATED PLAINLY: on Steven's phone (API 31+) this wins over the
  brand cream/navy in values/. If the wallpaper-tinted look is not wanted, delete
  this file and values-night-v31/widget_colors.xml and the brand pair applies at
  every API level. Nothing else references these names conditionally.
-->
<resources>
    <!-- Card surface + text. -->
    <color name="widget_bg">@android:color/system_neutral1_50</color>
    <color name="widget_ink">@android:color/system_neutral1_900</color>
    <color name="widget_ink_dim">@android:color/system_neutral2_700</color>
    <color name="widget_accent">@android:color/system_accent1_700</color>
    <color name="widget_track">@android:color/system_accent1_100</color>
    <color name="widget_good">@android:color/system_accent3_700</color>

    <!-- Week-bar chart, resolved in OUR process. See WidgetRenderer.barColors(). -->
    <color name="widget_bar_fill">@android:color/system_accent1_600</color>
    <!-- One step of emphasis, not a different element. At _900 the current week
         rendered as a near-black block that read as a separate control rather
         than the tallest bar in the same series. -->
    <color name="widget_bar_current">@android:color/system_accent1_800</color>
    <color name="widget_bar_track">@android:color/system_accent1_100</color>

    <!-- ═══ v108.2 — solar-style redesign, Material You LIGHT ═══════════════
         accent3 is Android's tertiary ramp, which is where the green-ish tone
         lives in a wallpaper-derived palette — the closest the system offers to
         the ring green in values/. As everywhere in this file the guarantee is
         TONAL DISTANCE, not a hex we can measure: _700 against a _50 surface is
         a 650-tone gap. The rendered PNGs are the empirical check. -->
    <!-- _200, not _100. At _100 the track measured 1.14:1 against the _50 card
         on a real API 34 device: the unfilled part of the ring was invisible, so
         a 3% ring read as a small green arc floating in space rather than as a
         circle 3% full. Caught by ringIsDistinguishableFromTheCardInBothThemes,
         which resolves the ACTUAL wallpaper tones rather than trusting the ramp
         names — the empirical check the v108.0 notes said these would need. -->
    <!-- widget_ring_fill is DELIBERATELY NOT OVERRIDDEN HERE, so it falls through
         to values/ (and values-night/) at every API level.

         Everything else on this card follows the wallpaper, which is what makes
         the widget sit in the home screen rather than on it. The ring does not,
         because the ring is the thing Steven actually asked for — "solar is way
         cooler, I like the circle progress graph" — and he asked for it GREEN.
         On a wallpaper-derived palette accent3_700 came out a dark plum, which
         renders as a bruise on the track rather than as progress.

         So: card and ink are Material You, the ring is brand. Adding the name
         back to this file is all it takes to hand the ring to the wallpaper too. -->
    <color name="widget_ring_track">@android:color/system_accent1_200</color>

    <color name="widget_pill_bg">@android:color/system_accent1_100</color>
    <color name="widget_pill_ink">@android:color/system_neutral1_900</color>

    <color name="widget_action_bg">@android:color/system_neutral2_100</color>
</resources>
